If you need to add a new endpoint follow steps 1-5, otherwise skip to step 6.

1. Add it to fixed.list or mobile.list as appropriate.

2. Save off a copy of the current /usr/testbed/tmp/syncthing:

   sudo cp -rp /usr/testbed/tmp/syncthing /usr/testbed/tmp/syncthing.old

3. Create a new version:

   cd <testbed-src>/powder/syncthing
   sudo genstconfig -M boss.list,fixed.list,mobile.list

4. Compare it to the old:

   diff -r /usr/testbed/tmp/syncthing.old /usr/testbed/tmp/syncthing

   The only difference should be new files for each endpoint you added
   and some added text to boss.emulab.net/config.xml.

5. Install the new boss syncthing config and restart

   sudo /usr/local/etc/rc.d/syncthing stop
   sudo cp /usr/testbed/tmp/syncthing/boss.emulab.net/config.xml \
       /usr/testbed/etc/syncthing/
   sudo /usr/local/etc/rc.d/syncthing start
   sudo /usr/local/etc/rc.d/syncthing status

6. Create the tarball for new configs:

   cd /usr/testbed/tmp/syncthing/boss.bus-XXXX.powderwireless.net
   sudo tar czf ../bus-XXXX.tar.gz .



To add collection of RF monitoring data from buses and other endpoints.

Mothership boss:

0. Pick a bus/endpoint, changing XXXX to the appropriate value:

   # FE or bus-XXXX
   aggregate=bookstore
   tarball=/usr/testbed/tmp/syncthing/$aggregate.tar.gz
   # endpoint or mobile
   type=endpoint

1. Copy over the appropriate tarball (TBD):

   sudo scp $tarball boss.$aggregate.powderwireless.net:/tmp/st.tar.gz

2. Create the mirror directory where aggregate data goes, along with the
   marker (only needed on new installs):

   sudo mkdir -p /usr/testbed/www/rfmonitor-$type/$aggregate/.stfolder
   sudo chown -R elabman:tbadmin /usr/testbed/www/rfmonitor-$type/$aggregate

Aggregate boss:

0. Connect

   sudo ssh boss.$aggregate.powderwireless.net
   
1. Enable the "global" syncthing daemon, append this to the aggregate boss
   node's /etc/rc.conf:

   sudo ed /etc/rc.conf
   $a

# Syncthing for Powder RF data collection
syncthing_enable="YES"
syncthing_home="/usr/testbed/etc/syncthing"
syncthing_log_file="/usr/testbed/log/syncthing.log"
syncthing_user="elabman"
syncthing_group="tbadmin"
.
   w
   q

2. Install the syncthing config for the aggregate boss.

   sudo mkdir -m 700 /usr/testbed/etc/syncthing
   sudo tar -C /usr/testbed/etc/syncthing -xzf /tmp/st.tar.gz
   sudo chown -R elabman:tbadmin /usr/testbed/etc/syncthing
   sudo rm /tmp/st.tar.gz

3. Mark the data directory so that syncthing knows we really intended it
   to operate on the directory:

   sudo mkdir /usr/testbed/www/rfmonitor/.stfolder
   sudo chown elabman:tbadmin /usr/testbed/www/rfmonitor/.stfolder

   Nothing goes into this directory, syncthing just makes sure it exists.
   If you start up syncthing and it doesn't appear to be doing anything
   (no files show up in the mirror directory on mothership boss), then
   it is probably because this subdir is missing.

3a. Restore old data if you have it.

   cd /usr/testbed/www
   sudo mv rfmonitor/.htaccess rfmonitor/.htaccess.new
   sudo tar xzf /tmp/rfmonitor.tar.gz
   sudo mv rfmonitor/.htaccess.new rfmonitor/.htaccess
   sudo rm /tmp/rfmonitor.tar.gz

   It is probably best to get things back in sync while the node is in
   Mike's office with good WiFi, to temporarily up the allowed send rate
   of the node by changing the send bandwith (maxSendKbps) in the config file
   (/usr/testbed/etc/syncthing/config.xml) from "500" (500Kbps) to "0"
   (unlimited).

4. Start em up and see if there was anything obviously wrong:

   sudo /usr/local/etc/rc.d/syncthing start
   sleep 1
   tail -50 /usr/testbed/log/syncthing.log

To monitor what is going on:

Syncthing has a GUI that listens just on localhost, but you can do a port
forward on your local machine if you want to look at it:

   ssh -N -L 9090:127.0.0.1:8384 boss.$aggregate.powderwireless.net &

and then go to "localhost:9090" in your browser.

You can do the same with the boss.emulab.net syncthing as well, running
a port forward on boss.emulab.net.

If you restored old data to the rfmonitor directory, this initial sync
could take a long time. After it is done you can change the send rate in
the config file back to 500:

        <maxSendKbps>500</maxSendKbps>

We should revisit this limit after we have switched our our buses over to
our own AT&T unlimited data plans. I would guess we should be able to do
at least 1000Kbps without affecting anything. We can do almost 10Mb/sec
via the WiFi in MEB.

